const semantic={
 'AP / WAP':{family:'wireless_access_point_vocabulary',qualification:'Qualify the explicitly expanded access-point vocabulary for an infrastructure WLAN connecting clients through a distribution system, commonly wired. The slash groups names for this device role rather than two independently assessed controls.',contrasts:['The supplied WAP expansion fixes the intended device meaning in this card; the acronym alone in another context is not sufficient to identify this role.','An AP is not inherently a router, firewall or secure configuration. Wired connection describes the common infrastructure case, not every possible mesh/backhaul topology; association does not prove authorization or segmentation.']},
 ARP:{family:'local_protocol_to_link_address_vocabulary',qualification:'Qualify the common IPv4/Ethernet local-link mapping from a protocol address to a link-layer address; this is vocabulary, not a packet capture, trustworthy binding or universal all-IP resolution mechanism.',contrasts:['ARP does not allocate an IP address like DHCP or resolve a domain name like DNS. Routing chooses the local next hop to resolve; a remote destination does not imply an end-to-end remote MAC lookup.','The sentence is bounded to the conventional IPv4/Ethernet case; IPv6 neighbor discovery and non-Ethernet variants are outside this card. No authenticated mapping, poisoning detection or successful exchange is established.']},
 DHCP:{family:'host_configuration_allocation_vocabulary',qualification:'Qualify automatic host address/configuration delivery as the usual DHCP role. Allocation can be automatic, dynamic or administrator-assigned; the card does not promise every address is a fresh dynamic lease.',contrasts:['DHCP supplies address/configuration parameters; ARP performs local link resolution and DNS name-data lookup. Static/manual allocation and lease renewal are not distinguished by a short name recall.','The inspected DHCPv4 background does not approve DHCPv6, guaranteed service availability or a trusted server. No lease, relay, rogue-server control or working configuration is tested.']},
 DMZ:{family:'screened_subnet_vocabulary',qualification:'Qualify the screened subnet/public-service separation pattern as an architecture description, conditional on actual routing and filtering policy rather than guaranteed safety from the label.',contrasts:['Public service placement can reduce direct exposure of internal systems, but permitted flows, hardened hosts and enforced boundaries determine protection. Separate subnet labels alone do not establish effective isolation.','A DMZ is not necessarily an air gap or a single mandated topology. The card supplies no designed policy, permitted-flow analysis or secure deployed architecture.']},
 DNS:{family:'domain_address_lookup_vocabulary',qualification:'Qualify domain-name-to-address lookup as one common DNS function, without narrowing the Domain Name System to only IP translations or promising a unique/live answer.',contrasts:['DNS associates typed data with names; multiple records, aliases, cache state or no answer may occur. It is different from DHCP address allocation and ARP local-link address resolution.','The short definition does not teach authoritative/recursive roles, validate returned data, prove DNSSEC, or imply encrypted queries. No lookup, record change or available service is executed.']},
 FACL:{family:'filesystem_permission_list_vocabulary',qualification:'Qualify the explicitly expanded filesystem access-control list as permissions for files/directories. This is an object-access vocabulary item in a broad deck, not a network forwarding ACL.',contrasts:['Filesystem ACL semantics depend on the platform, identity, permissions and inheritance model. The inspected Linux access/default ACL behavior is illustrative; it does not establish the semantics of every operating system.','ACL entries are not encryption, network packet rules or proof of effective least privilege. No access evaluation, inheritance change or privileged-user restriction is performed.']},
 'FTPS / SFTP':{family:'tls_ftp_vs_ssh_file_transfer_vocabulary',qualification:'Qualify the explicit transport distinction: FTPS adds TLS to FTP, while SFTP uses SSH transport. These are different file-transfer protocols, not interchangeable labels for FTP over SSH.',contrasts:['TLS/SSH protection depends on negotiated configuration and endpoint verification. FTPS control and data protection are separate policy choices; its historical TLS reference does not recommend obsolete TLS versions.','SFTP does not become FTPS by using an FTP-like interface, and neither implies protected storage or uncompromised endpoints. No connection, transfer, certificate or host key is verified; the grouped card adds no independent family.']},
 ICMP:{family:'ip_control_error_echo_vocabulary',qualification:'Qualify IP control/error messages with echo-based ping as a familiar example. The definition names a broader protocol role rather than treating every ICMP message as ping.',contrasts:['ICMP is not a TCP/UDP application service. Error reporting and echo replies do not guarantee delivery; filtering or absent replies do not establish that a host is down.','The inspected ICMPv4 introduction bounds this review; ICMPv6 neighbor discovery and router behavior are outside the short card. No reachability, attack or filtering policy is observed.']},
 IPsec:{family:'ip_security_suite_capability_vocabulary',qualification:'Qualify authentication/integrity and encryption as capabilities of the IPsec suite, commonly used in VPNs. The sentence is a suite-level summary, not a guarantee that every IPsec packet or association uses encryption.',contrasts:['AH offers integrity/origin authentication without confidentiality; ESP can also operate without encryption. Effective services depend on protocol, association mode, endpoints and selected options.','Transport/tunnel deployment, traffic selectors, keys and policy require separate decisions. No active VPN, protected flow, algorithm recommendation or complete architecture competency follows from vocabulary recall.']},
 LAN:{family:'local_area_network_vocabulary',qualification:'Qualify a geographically limited local network such as a home/office; geography alone does not prescribe medium, trust boundary or secure design.',contrasts:['LAN can use wired or wireless links and span a building/campus; it is not synonymous with a single VLAN, subnet or trusted security zone.','The inspected historical IEEE overview supports local-area terminology only, not current standards conformance, throughput, availability or a deployed network. No segmentation or access policy is evaluated.']},
 LDAP:{family:'directory_query_update_vocabulary',qualification:'Qualify the directory-access protocol role for requesting searches and modifications. The service applies authorization/schema restrictions; query/update capability does not imply an unrestricted successful operation.',contrasts:['LDAP is a protocol rather than the directory database or one specific vendor product. Directory attributes, authentication and authorization are separate concerns; the short definition proves none of them.','LDAP is not automatically encrypted. A successfully negotiated TLS layer or other suitable protection is separate from a basic query/update operation; no binding, search, update or credential protection is verified.']},
 MPLS:{family:'label_forwarding_vocabulary',qualification:'Qualify label-based forwarding in the MPLS architecture, interpreting the broad routing phrase as a forwarding method rather than a separate universal routing protocol.',contrasts:['Labels identify forwarding equivalence classes with local significance; the label need not encode an IP address. Control-plane route/path establishment remains separate from label forwarding.','A label or provider private-network service is not cryptographic confidentiality or automatically secure isolation. No route, label distribution, forwarding table, VPN or performance result is tested.']},
 'NAT / PAT':{family:'address_vs_address_port_translation_vocabulary',qualification:'Qualify the common traditional private-to-public translation case; PAT/NAPT additionally maps transport identifiers to distinguish flows sharing an address. The definition is a common example rather than the complete universe of NAT deployments.',contrasts:['Basic address translation and address-plus-port translation differ; PAT rewrites/maps ports rather than merely observing them. Mapping state and permitted return traffic are distinct from routing and address allocation.','NAT can involve other address realms and is not inherently encryption or a firewall policy. The card proves neither all ports are safe nor all inbound traffic is blocked; no translation table or real flow is evaluated.']}
};assert.deepEqual(Object.keys(semantic),terms);
